{"id":1989,"date":"2020-12-10T17:07:06","date_gmt":"2020-12-11T01:07:06","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1989"},"modified":"2024-09-01T15:51:13","modified_gmt":"2024-09-01T22:51:13","slug":"codrone-python-timers","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-python-timers\/","title":{"rendered":"Timers"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22158e9c7cd\" name=\"6a22158e9c7cd\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>process_time()<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You&#8217;ll be using the time.process_time() function to keep track of time in your programs. The best way to explain what it does is to see it in action in PyCharm.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import time\r\nwhile True:\r\n    print(time.process_time())\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Keep in mind that this is an infinite loop (see the &#8220;while True&#8221;?) so you will need to terminate your program.<\/p>\n<p>What do you notice? The numbers should be increasing at a familiar rate. The value of <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">time.process_time()<\/code>returns the number of seconds that have passed since running your programming.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22158e9c922\" name=\"6a22158e9c922\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Using process_time() with CoDrone<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]One way you can use timers: perform a sequence of flight events!<\/p>\n<p>To keep track of how much time has passed you will need two variables: one to hold the initial time and one to hold the final time. The difference of these two values will give you how much time has passed. First, import the necessary libraries (CoDrone and time) in your program and establish a connection with your CoDrone.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone\r\nimport time\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Next, pair to your drone.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Before your drone takes off, it needs a variable to start the time! Use <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">begintime = time.process_time()<\/code>. In this program, you&#8217;re just going to measure how long takeoff takes, so add <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">endtime = time.process_time()<\/code>after takeoff.<\/p>\n<p>To subtract begin_time from end_time, you will need to include <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">actual_time = end_time - begin_time<\/code>, along with a print command for actual_time.<\/p>\n<p>Finally, end your program by landing and disconnecting your drone. This is what your code should look like:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import time\r\nimport CoDrone\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\nbegin_time = time.process_time()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nend_time = time.process_time()\r\n\r\nactual_time = end_time - begin_time\r\nprint(actual_time)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.disconnect()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22158e9c99f\" name=\"6a22158e9c99f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Programming assignment<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Let\u2019s reuse the square code from lesson <a href=\"https:\/\/drive.google.com\/drive\/folders\/18IKamAEF9Da_O8q0GG_CxXG3skm-LLd2?usp=sharing\"><u>2B: Fli<\/u>g<u>ht Movements<\/u><\/a>, but this time, you&#8217;ll put that code in a function and add timers to see how long the square code lasts. You\u2019ll need to declare three variables: <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">begin_time, end_time<\/code>, and <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">total_time<\/code>. The variables <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">begin_time<\/code>and <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">end_time<\/code>will be equal to <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">time.process_time()<\/code>. The location of these two variables is important! Since these variables belong before and aher the square code, that is where you\u2019ll get the most accurate time. Once you get the values for <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">begin_time<\/code>and <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">end_time<\/code>, use <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">total_time<\/code>to find the difference between them, which is also the total runtime for the square.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import time\r\nimport CoDrone\r\ndrone = CoDrone.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair(drone.Nearest)\r\n\r\ndef square():\r\n            \r\n    drone.set_pitch(30)\t#\tforward set at 30% power\r\n    drone.move(2)\t#\tforward for 2 seconds\r\n    drone.set_pitch(0) \r\n            \r\n    drone.set_roll(-30)\t#\tleft set at 30% power\r\n    drone.move(2)\t#\tleft for 2 seconds\r\n    drone.set_roll(0)    \r\n    drone.set_pitch(-30)#\tbackward set at 30% power\r\n    drone.move(2)\t#\tbackward for 2 seconds\r\n    drone.set_pitch(0)\r\n            \r\n    drone.set_roll(30)\t#\tright set at 30% power \r\n    drone.move(2)\t# right for 2 seconds\r\n    drone.set_roll(0)\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\nbegin_time = time.process_time()\r\nsquare()\r\nend_time = time.process_time()\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\ntotal_time = end_time - begin_time\r\nprint(total_time, \"seconds\")\r\n\r\ndrone.close()\t# disconnects CoDrone<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22158e9c9f6\" name=\"6a22158e9c9f6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>If statements and process_time()<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You can also use timers to activate actions with if statements. However, if there are multiple if, elif, and else statements, then you&#8217;ll need a while loop to continually check for updated conditions.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import time\r\nimport CoDrone\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair(drone.Nearest)\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.hover(2)\r\n\r\nwhile True:\r\n    # declare a variable to get time\r\n    current_time = time.process_time()\r\n\r\n    #print variable\r\n    print(current_time)\r\n \r\n    if current_time &lt; 10:\r\n        drone.set_yaw(20)\r\n        drone.move(3)\r\n        print(\"yaw\")\r\n    elif current_time &lt; 20:\r\n        drone.set_roll(10)\r\n        drone.move(3)\r\n        print(\"roll\")\r\n    elif current_time &lt; 30:\r\n        drone.land()\r\n        drone.disconnect()\r\n    else:\r\n        # escape while loop\r\n        break<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22158e9ca4f\" name=\"6a22158e9ca4f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Programming challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Make a code that will do an action based on how much time has passed. Have your CoDrone take off and then do an action, like pitch, for 2 seconds. Then see how much time has passed and print that time. Next, create an if statement that checks if the time is less than a certain amount of time. If the condition is satisfied, do another action or two. Finally, land and disconnect.<\/p>\n<p>Below is an example:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import time\r\nimport CoDrone\r\nfrom CoDrone import Color, Mode\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair(drone.Nearest)\r\ndrone.takeoff() \r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(10)\r\ndrone.move(2)\r\n\r\nclock = time.process_time()\r\nprint(clock)\r\nif time.process_time() &lt; 20:\r\n    drone.set_arm_led(Color.Yellow, Mode.SOLID, 20)\r\n    drone.set_throttle(25)\r\n    drone.move(3)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22158e9cabd\" name=\"6a22158e9cabd\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<strong>Challenge: Two Minute Code<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Create a code that can last for two minutes!<\/p>\n<p><strong>Rules:<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Every fifteen or twenty seconds, the CoDrone has to do a different action.<\/li>\n<li>Use a while loop that checks if time.process_time() is less than 180.<\/li>\n<li>Use if, elif, and else statements that check what time it is.<\/li>\n<li>Every 15-20 seconds, your program should do a different color, CoDrone action, or print a string.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Below is an example. It&#8217;s just missing flight commands!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import time\r\nimport CoDrone\r\nfrom CoDrone import Color, Mode\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair(drone.Nearest)\r\n\r\nwhile time.clock() &lt; 120:\r\n    current_time = time.clock() \r\n    print(current_time)\r\n    if current_time &lt; 20:\r\n        drone.set_arm_led(Color.Yellow, Mode.DIMMING, 20)\r\n    elif current_time &lt; 40:\r\n        drone.set_arm_led(Color.Blue, Mode.SOLID, 20)\r\n    elif current_time &lt; 60:\r\n        drone.set_arm_led(Color.Orange, Mode.REVERSE_PULSE, 20)\r\n    elif current_time &lt; 80:\r\n        drone.set_arm_led(Color.Purple, Mode.DIMMING, 20)\r\n    elif current_time &lt; 100:\r\n        drone.set_arm_led(Color.Red, Mode.STROBE, 20)\r\n    elif current_time &lt; 120:\r\n        drone.set_arm_led(Color.Green, Mode.PULSE, 20)\r\n        print(\"done\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 7 process_time() [vc_column_text]You'll be using the time.process_time() function to keep track of time in your programs. The best way to explain what it does is to see it&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-python-timers\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1759,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":808,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2164,2156,2122,2134,2139],"class_list":["post-1989","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-codrone","lesson-tag-intermediate","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-timers","module-intermediate","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1989","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":5,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1989\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":3551,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1989\/revisions\/3551"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1759"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1989"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=1989"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=1989"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}